One concern: non-transaction queries (e.g., `createMeeting` uses `db.query` directly, not a transaction) are not flushed with syncToFs. The comment says "PGlite 0.5.16 flushes to IndexedDB after BEGIN but not after COMMIT" — so single statements executed outside explicit transactions (auto-commit) may also not be flushed until the next statement? The `createMeeting`, `updateMeeting`, `deleteMeeting`, `deleteAll` all use `db.query` directly (not `db.transaction`). If PGlite doesn't flush after COMMIT (implicit transactions commit after each statement when not in explicit transaction? Actually PGlite runs each statement in its own implicit transaction unless in an explicit one). The claim in the comment: "flushes to IndexedDB after BEGIN but not after COMMIT, so a committed transaction stays in memory until the next statement, and a reload loses it". If that's true for implicit transactions too, then `createMeeting`, `updateMeeting` (which writes the leftAt — critical for WC-7 "end time must reach storage before the person can reload"), `deleteMeeting`, `deleteAll` would all be lost on reload. This looks like a genuine data-loss defect: `updateMeeting` is the write that persists the meeting end time, and it goes through `db.query` without `syncToFs`.

