### pglite-history-repository.ts — run() error handling

```js
const run = async (signal, work) => {
  signal?.throwIfAborted();
  const db = await database();
  ...
  try {
    const value = await work(db);
    signal?.throwIfAborted();
    return ok(value);
  } catch (error) {
    if (signal?.aborted === true) throw signal.reason;
    console.error(...);
    return err(historyErrors.storageFailed());
  }
};
```

Note: `signal?.throwIfAborted()` before opening DB. After work, throwIfAborted — abort after successful work throws, discarding the value. That matches "a fired signal rejects the promise" contract. But the write already happened — that's fine, contract says abort isn't a storage failure.

But `runWrite`:

```js
const runWrite = async (work) => {
  const outcome = await run(undefined, work);
  return outcome.ok ? outcome.value : outcome;
};
```

Here `outcome` is `Result<Result<T, HistoryEntryNotFound>, HistoryStorageError>`. If outer ok → value is `Result<T, HistoryEntryNotFound>`; return it (correct: HistoryWriteError = union). If outer !ok → outcome is the error result of type `Result<..., HistoryStorageError>` which structurally is `{ ok: false, error: HistoryStorageError }` — assignable to `Result<T, HistoryWriteError>`. OK, works.

### listMeetings pagination bug

```js
const page = await tx.query<SessionRow>(NEWEST_FIRST_PAGE, [position?.joinedAt ?? null, position?.id ?? null, limit + 1]);
const summaries = page.rows.map(toMeetingSummary).filter((m) => m !== null);
const shown = summaries.slice(0, limit);
const last = shown.at(-1);
const hasMore = page.rows.length > limit && last !== undefined;
```

Bug: if some rows fail `toMeetingSummary` (return null, filtered out), `page.rows.length > limit` still computed from raw rows. Suppose limit+1 rows fetched, one is invalid → summaries has limit rows... shown = all of them, hasMore = true, cursor from last. OK. But if more than one invalid: e.g., limit=50, fetched 51, 3 invalid → summaries=48, shown=48, hasMore=true, fine. If fetched ≤ limit rows (last page), hasMore=false — but some invalid rows were dropped; fine. Edge: hasMore uses raw count which is correct-ish. The `nextCursor` from `shown.at(-1)` — if all summaries filtered out (all invalid) but page.rows.length > limit, last === undefined → hasMore=false even though more pages exist. Minor edge. Not a big deal.

Another pagination subtlety: cursor uses `last.joinedAt, last.id` where `last` is from `shown` (post-filter). Rows with joinedAt < cursor handled. Filtering happens client-side; rows skipped could be re-fetched if cursor points past them? No — cursor is from the last shown row; next page fetches rows strictly after cursor; skipped invalid rows are before cursor, not refetched. Fine.

`total: toNumber(total.rows[0]?.total) ?? shown.length` — total as fallback to shown.length is weird but harmless.

Actually wait, one more thing: `toNumber` on count from PGlite — `count(*)` returns bigint; PGlite returns bigint as string typically. toNumber handles string. OK.

